{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And YHWH Elohim caused to sprout from the ground.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the verses prove that the Garden of Eden is on earth, and there are the Tree of Life and the Tree of Knowledge. The Tree of Life grants long life to those who eat of it, and the Tree of Knowledge grants to those who eat of it will and choice, whether for intellectual matters or for bodily matters. And thus the language proves that it refers to will and choice, and this is the language of our Rabbis, of blessed memory: 'nothing but that his da'at was to return'; and likewise the language of the verse (Psalms 144:3): ״מה אדם ותדעהו״ — 'what is man that You should know him,' that is, what is his nature that You should desire him; and if so, the explanation of 'the Tree of Knowledge' is as if it said 'the Tree of Will,' and the Holy One, blessed be He, withheld it from him because man was compelled in his deeds before he sinned, and all his actions were pure intellect, like the angel of YHWH, who is compelled to perform all his actions intellectually because there is no impediment within him — so too was man; but after he sinned and ate of the Tree of Knowledge, he had will and choice to know good and evil, and he became clothed in bodily desires. And to this the word וידעו alludes, as it says: ״ותפקחנה עיני שניהם וידעו״ — 'and the eyes of both of them were opened and they knew' — that is, they drew will and choice from the Tree of Knowledge, and the choice was in their hands to do evil or to do good. This is the attribute of Elohim — a measure that is good from one side and evil from the other side, for through it the first man had within it impulse and desire." "<b>And the Tree of Life in the midst of the garden.</b> The verse explains of the Tree of Life that it was in the midst of the garden, in its very center, and it did not do so for the Tree of Knowledge, for it did not specify in which place of the garden it was." "<b>However,</b> from the fact that it mentioned 'in the midst of the garden,' and placed it between the Tree of Life and the Tree of Knowledge, we understand that both of them were certainly within the garden. And even though they are two, and it is impossible for two trees to be in the exact center, here they were one below and two above: for their joining was one below, and they go and separate above, and thus both of them were in the very midst of the garden. And the verse goes on and tells of the rivers of the Garden of Eden, its borders, and the lands surrounding it, and it informs us that it has four rivers, and those four rivers go out and are drawn from one river, and that river is the one that goes out from Eden, and it is what waters the garden, and from the garden it divides and separates into four heads. And you already know that all these matters in the Garden of Eden are on earth, and all of them are depictions and likenesses for the intellectual matters in the supernal Garden of Eden." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> the four rivers correspond to the four kingdoms. 'The name of the first is Pishon' — this is the kingdom of Babylon, because it is written (Habakkuk 1:8): ״ופשו פרשיו״ (and their horsemen spread out); 'that is the one that surrounds all the land of Havilah' — it rose and encompassed all the land of Israel, as it is said (Psalms 42:6): ״הוחילי לאלהים כי עוד אודנו״ — 'hope in Elohim, for I will yet praise Him' (i.e., 'wait' — חילה, related to הוחילי). 'Where the gold is' — these are the words of Torah, as it is said (Psalms 19:11): ״הנחמדים מזהב ומפז רב״ (more desirable than gold, than much fine gold). 'And the gold of that land is good' — this teaches that there is no Torah like the Torah of the land of Israel, and no wisdom like the wisdom of the land of Israel. 'There is the bdolium and the onyx stone' — there are Scripture, Mishnah, Talmud, Toseftot, and Aggadot. 'And the name of the second river is Gihon' — this is Media. 'Hiddekel' — this is Greece, which was sharp (חדה) and swift (קלה) in its decrees. 'And the fourth river, that is Perat' — this is the kingdom of Edom, which was fruitful (הפרה) and pressed (הצרה) before it, etc. Perat, after its end, as it is said (Isaiah 63:3): ״פורה דרכתי לבדי״ (I have trodden the winepress alone)." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> the Tree of Life and the Tree of Knowledge above are Tiferet and Atarah, and man sinned in deed and in thought in the Tree of Knowledge and in its likeness." "<b>And behold,</b> Adam in his sin did not deny the specific Name, for even though he saw the Tree of Life before him, he supposed the Tree of Knowledge was the essence and followed after it, believing that it was the essence, since it was 'good and evil,' embracing all opposites, and that all actions in the supernal and the nether realms were performed through it. And for this reason Scripture mentioned 'fruit' in connection with the Tree of Knowledge, but you will not find so in connection with the Tree of Life. And his sin was that he cut down the plantings — that is, he severed the power of the specific Name that enters into the planting, and this caused the planting to wither and be corrupted, so that the planting was worthy of taking vengeance upon him. Therefore he was punished with death, that his soul should separate from his body at his death, corresponding to his having separated the supernal power from the planting. And in this was the sin of Nadab and Abihu, who were punished through the planting — this is ״ותצא אש״; and likewise Elisha ben Abuyah, who was given permission to destroy them — the merit of 'Aḥer.' But the generation of the Dispersion denied the specific Name; therefore they were punished through it, as it says ״וירד ה׳״, and they were not punished through the planting, because the vengeance belonged to the great Name, as I shall mention there, with the help of Heaven. And when he repented and saw that he was receiving from above from the attribute of the left, he then brought his offering — an ox — corresponding to that attribute in which he had caused a blemish, for it receives from the left. And 'river' (נהר) is from the language of the Targum אור נהורא — and this is the light that comes from the Infinite (אין סוף); this is the meaning of ״מעדן״ — which means from the channel (מסרת), since from there onward there is no boundary or apprehension, that one could say of it 'up to such-and-such a place.' And we find a parallel (Job 25:5): ״הן עד ירח ולא יאהיל״ — for it is in the power of the Almighty to remove the light of the moon so that its light does not shine; but that light coming from the Infinite never ceases, neither by day nor by night, and for this reason it says ״יוצא״ (going forth) and did not say ״יצא״ (went forth). ״To water the garden״ — this is the Crown (כתר), and you already know that all the Sefirot, all of them, up to the Crown, are unified: there is no severing and no separation at all; but from there onward extends the world of separate intelligences — this is the meaning of ״ומשם יפרד״, for from there the world of separate intelligences begins. And it ״had four heads״ — the four camps of the Shekhinah, which draw power to the four rivers of Paradise and draw power to the four kingdoms upon earth. And may the Place enlighten our eyes with the light of His Torah."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :register {:flags {5 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {3 "The prooftext from Psalms 42:6 (הוחילי לאלהים) is brought as a wordplay linking חילה/הוחילי to the idea of waiting or hoping in the land of Israel; the verse citation is rendered exactly as given.", 5 "The comment opens with the lemma והנה from Gen. 2:9 but ranges widely across multiple verses of the chapter; the trailing prayer at the end appears to close the comment rather than lead into the next."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:09:38.842753583Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}